mimid-chat: an interactive command-line chat client over MIMI protocol-07,
for the IETF 127 hackathon table (bead mimid-1wgh.46.14). A throwaway
interop tool, not mimid's client design: that is mimid-agent (bead
mimid-1wgh.13). One process is one user, mimi://<own-domain>/u/<name>,
talking to one hub, Haven's mimi-hubd at haven.reviewcommit.com by default.
None of this runs in CI.

Layout
  Cargo.toml     standalone crate (own [workspace]): mimid crates by relative
                 path, plus a copy of the root Cargo.toml [patch] section
                 (keep the two in step when the openmls pins move)
  src/main.rs    arguments (clap), the command loop, the line editor
                 (rustyline), /sleep and /expect
  src/chat.rs    rooms as MLS groups, the commands, incoming items; its
                 module comment says how messages travel
  src/hub.rs     the hub: protocol-07 wire lane through mimid's PeerClient,
                 and mimi-hubd's JSON lane (/mimi/v1/*)
  src/jmap.rs    JMAP to the user's own mimid server (--jmap-url)
  src/state.rs   the state directory
  chat.sh        runs mimid-chat interactively on the mimid host over ssh -t
  acceptance.sh  the two-user acceptance test on the mimid host

Build and copy to the mimid host
  export CARGO_TARGET_DIR=/data/cargo/mimid-chat-target
  (cd $MIMID/tools/interop/chat && cargo build --release)
  scp -i ~/.ssh/mimid-hackathon.pem -o BatchMode=yes \
     $CARGO_TARGET_DIR/release/mimid-chat admin@172.31.40.235:/home/admin/interop/chat/
  The mimid host's outbound proxy (haven.reviewcommit.com is 127.0.0.3 in its
  /etc/hosts) adds mimid's mTLS client certificate, which names
  mimid.reviewcommit.com; /home/admin/interop/trust.pem trusts the hub. So
  mimid-chat runs there, not on this dev box.

JMAP mode, against your own mimid server (bead mimid-1wgh.12.41)
  With --jmap-url and --jmap-token-file, mimid-chat talks to the user's own
  mimid server over JMAP (draft-atwood-jmap-chat-mimi-00) instead of the wire
  and JSON lanes: KeyPackage/set to publish, Room/create for /host,
  KeyMaterial/fetch and Room/update for /invite and other changes, Room/send
  for messages. /host then makes a draft-conformant room ID (roomIdPrefix
  and base32 of 16 random octets) and a random 16-octet group ID, passes
  the room ID to Room/create, and puts the name in the room metadata. With
  --no-meta it leaves the metadata out, so users whose clients do not
  support room_metadata (0x0023) can be invited: a room with metadata
  requires every member to list it (mls-extensions-11 §5). The account and token come from the
  server's admin CLI:
    mimid admin mimid.toml add-user mimi://a.example/u/alice
    mimid admin mimid.toml token mimi://a.example/u/alice   # {"token": ...}
    mimid-chat --user alice --own-domain a.example --hub a.example \
      --hub-url http://127.0.0.1:18080 --jmap-url http://127.0.0.1:18080 \
      --jmap-token-file alice.token --state-dir state-alice
  Receiving is by polling: every Message the first time, then
  Message/changes; each body is a FanoutMessage, handled as one from the hub,
  and the client's own handshakes and messages (which the server stores too)
  are skipped by a digest kept in the state. A message for a room not joined
  yet is held and replayed at /join. Tried locally: alice hosts and invites
  bob and carol, each joins from the Welcome, and messages go both ways.
  Consent goes through Consent/set (bead mimid-1wgh.12.43): /allow and
  /deny record your grant or refusal, /request and /cancel ask or stop
  asking, and the server sends each to the other user's provider, any
  provider, not just the hub's. /consents lists your records, including
  requests others sent you.

Running it (one terminal per user)
  From this dev box:
    ssh -t -i ~/.ssh/mimid-hackathon.pem admin@172.31.40.235 \
      SSL_CERT_FILE=/home/admin/interop/trust.pem \
      /home/admin/interop/chat/mimid-chat --user alice \
      --state-dir /home/admin/interop/chat/state-alice
  or the same through the wrapper:
    ./chat.sh alice
    ./chat.sh carol --poll-ms 1000
    RUST_LOG=mimid_chat=debug ./chat.sh alice     # logs go to stderr

  Options (mimid-chat --help; defaults in brackets):
    --user NAME          a-z 0-9 . _ -, up to 64
    --state-dir DIR      created 0700; files 0600
    --hub DOMAIN         [haven.reviewcommit.com]
    --hub-url URL        where the directory is fetched [https://HUB]
    --lane-url URL       mimi-hubd's JSON lane [https://HUB:8443]
    --own-domain DOMAIN  [mimid.reviewcommit.com]; the proxy's client
                         certificate must name it
    --poll-ms MS         100 to 600000 [2000]
    --no-publish         publish KeyPackages only on /publish, not at start-up
                       or after a join (for a hub that asks visitors not to
                       write through its JSON lane)
  --script FILE        read commands from FILE, echoing each as "> line";
                         lines starting with # are skipped
  A bad value is refused with a usage message and exit status 2.

  On a terminal (ssh -t, as above) input is read with rustyline:
    - the prompt names the current room, e.g. "[table]> ", or "> " for none;
    - incoming lines print above the prompt, and a half-typed line is
      redrawn intact below them;
    - history (up/down arrows) is kept in DIR/history, mode 0600. It holds
      the messages you typed in plaintext, like the rest of DIR;
    - Ctrl-C clears the line, Ctrl-D on an empty line (or /quit) exits.
  When stdin or stdout is not a terminal, lines are read plainly, one per
  line, and output goes to stdout as is. --script never uses rustyline.

  A typical session, alice and carol in two terminals:
    carol> /allow alice          lets alice fetch carol's KeyPackages
    alice> /create table         mimi://haven.reviewcommit.com/r/table
    alice> /invite carol
    carol>                       * invite #1 arrived: /join 1 to accept
    carol> /join
    alice> hello                 any line not starting with / is a message
    carol>                       [table] alice@mimid.reviewcommit.com: hello
    alice> /leave

Commands
  /create <name>     room mimi://<hub>/r/<name>, you its first participant:
                     addParticipant on the JSON lane (as b4.sh does), then an
                     MLS group whose ID is the room URI, with app_components
                     naming participant_list, roles_list and
                     base_room_policy, and a list of just you
  /adopt <name>      as /create, without addParticipant: for a room the hub
                     already lists you in, such as one its operator seeded
                     (Haven's r/table at the IETF 127 table)
  /invite <user>     a mimi:// URI, or a bare name on your domain. keyMaterial
                     (§5.2) for the user; a Commit with the Add and one
                     ParticipantListUpdate (mimi-hubd takes exactly one
                     change per Commit, DIV-10), sent with update (§5.3);
                     then the Welcome and the Commit are delivered (below)
  /invite <user> --policy k=v ... | --roles
                     the same Commit also carries a base_room_policy or a
                     roles_list AppDataUpdate (room-policy-04 §5, §3), to
                     test whether a hub takes a policy change beside a
                     participant change: §5 allows it, §3 does not, and
                     mimi-hubd refuses both (DIV-10, one change per Commit)
  /roles             a Commit with one roles_list update: role 1 "banned"
                     and role 2 "member", the role mimid-chat gives every
                     participant. mimi-hubd refuses a base_room_policy for a
                     room with no roles, and requires role 1 to be "banned"
  /roles ban | ban-no-banned
                     as /roles, and the member role also holds canBan with
                     a change from role 2 to role 1; ban-no-banned leaves
                     role 1 undefined (room-policy-04 §8.1.3: a ban needs it)
  /roles no-ban | no-add
                     as /roles ban with canBan taken away (the 2->1 change
                     still listed), or as /roles without canAddParticipant:
                     a hub must refuse /ban or /invite by that member
                     (§8.1; the committer-authority check)
  /ban <user>        a Commit that moves <user> to role 1 in the participant
                     list and removes all their clients (§8.1.3 canBan;
                     protocol-07 §7.6 changedRoleParticipants)
  /policy k=v ...    a Commit with one base_room_policy update, starting
                     from the room's current policy: fixed_membership,
                     multi_device, pseudonyms_allowed, persistent_room,
                     discoverable (true|false), max_clients, max_users (a
                     number or none)
  /allow <user>      updateConsent grant (§5.7) with you as target: the hub
                     serves your KeyPackages only to users you allowed
  /join [n]          accept pending invite n (default the oldest). Messages
                     that arrived for the room before the join are replayed
  /rooms, /room X    list rooms; switch the current room (name or URI)
  /who               the participant list (§7.5) and the MLS leaves
  /leave [room]      §3.5: an AppDataUpdate proposal removing you, sent with
                     update; the hub holds it, and the remaining member with
                     the lowest leaf commits it with the Remove of your leaf
  /publish [n]       publish n more KeyPackages (start-up and each join keep
                     about 5 published)
  /poll              pull now
  /sleep S           (scripts) pause the command stream; polling continues
  /expect TEXT [S]   (scripts) wait up to S seconds (60) for an output line
                     containing TEXT; on time-out, report it and exit 1 at end
  /quit              also end of input
  Text is sent as mimi-content-10 (text/plain, senderUri and roomUri set),
  encrypted with MLS, with submitMessage (§5.4). Received text has control,
  bidi and zero-width characters escaped, and a senderUri or roomUri that
  does not match the MLS sender or the room is refused (mimi-content-10
  §3.3).

How things reach a user when the hub never dials out
  mimi-hubd sends no /notify, fans a submitted message out only to the
  participants on its own domain (DIV-5), and neither stores nor relays
  Commits. It does keep a deliver-once FIFO queue per routing key on its
  JSON lane: POST /mimi/v1/submitMessage?recipient=K deposits opaque bytes,
  GET /mimi/v1/message?user=K pulls the next one. So:
  - every client polls GET /mimi/v1/message?user=<own URI>;
  - whoever sends anything deposits a copy for each other participant. For a
    user on another domain (every mimid user) the copy is the protocol-07
    §5.5 FanoutMessage the hub would have sent that user's provider in a
    /notify (application, proposal, commit or welcome). For a user on the
    hub's domain it is the bare MLSMessage, and application messages are
    left to the hub's own fan-out;
  - a Welcome goes twice: through the hub's Welcome relay (POST
    /mimi/v1/welcome/ingest?recipient=, which runs the hub's suite gate;
    pulled with GET /mimi/v1/welcome?user=), and as a FanoutMessage welcome
    in the invitee's message queue. Only the second carries the ratchet tree
    (RatchetTreeOption full): mimid's GroupInfo leaves the ratchet_tree
    extension out (§5.3) and the hub keeps no tree. The client keeps
    whichever copy comes first and fills in the tree from the other;
  - submitMessage also queues each message under the room URI, which nobody
    reads and which holds at most 256 items before the hub refuses
    submitMessage, so every member drains the room's queue and discards it;
  - the Commit removing a leaver is also deposited for the leaver, whose
    client then marks the room left.
  The consent and KeyPackage parts are the hub's own: KeyPackages are
  published on the JSON lane (POST /mimi/v1/keyMaterial/ingest?user=) and
  served by the wire keyMaterial route only to requesters the target
  allowed.

What a Haven user (Jason's client) needs to join a mimid-created room
  - KeyPackages published at the hub, and an updateConsent grant to the mimid
    inviter (the hub's own consent gate);
  - the Welcome: it is relayed to them (GET /mimi/v1/welcome?user=), but
    without the ratchet tree. Their client must either read the FanoutMessage
    welcome (mimid-chat deposits it only for users on other domains today;
    sending it to hub users too is a small change in invite() and
    distribute()), or the hub
    must keep the UpdateRequest's ratchetTreeOption and hand it out;
  - Commits and proposals: mimid-chat deposits the bare MLSMessages in their
    queue; their client must process them from it;
  - their messages reaching mimid users: the hub's submitMessage fans out to
    its own domain only, so either their client deposits a copy (bare
    MLSMessage or FanoutMessage; mimid-chat reads both) in each mimid
    participant's queue, or mimi-hubd's submit_message_wire also enqueues for
    plan.foreign (the participants fanout_targets already computes);
  - nothing about the room itself: Haven already holds the participant list
    mimid's Commits change, so their submitMessage is authorized.
  A mimid user joining a Haven-created room works the other way round only if
  the Haven client sends the Welcome and the tree by one of the routes above.

Acceptance test
  BIN=$CARGO_TARGET_DIR/release/mimid-chat ./acceptance.sh [A C ROOM]
  copies the binary to the host and drives two processes with --script, in
  two phases with both restarted in between. Phase 1: A creates the room, C
  allows A, A invites C, C joins, each sends a message and sees the other's.
  Phase 2: both reload from their state directories and exchange a message
  each, C's bad commands are refused, A leaves and C commits the leave. The
  default names carry a timestamp tag, so reruns start fresh. Outputs stay in
  /home/admin/interop/chat/acc/ on the host.

Limitations
  - Every proposal from a member is taken as that member's leave: it is the
    only proposal mimid-chat sends. A leave and an /invite in the same epoch
    make a Commit with two changes, which the hub refuses.
  - The queues are deliver-once: an item pulled by a process that dies before
    processing it is lost, and two processes for one user steal each other's
    items. Run one process per user.
  - No ordering or retry: if two members commit in the same epoch, the hub
    takes both (it never answers wrongEpoch), each refuses the other's
    Commit, and messages between them stop decrypting. Nothing repairs the
    group; recreate the room.
  - KeyPackages at the hub are counted, not queried. If an inviter cannot get
    one, the invitee runs /publish. Wiping a state directory leaves its old
    KeyPackages at the hub, served first: the next Welcomes for that user
    fail to open until those are used up. Use a new user name instead.
  - The keyMaterial request is signed with a fresh key per request, not the
    MLS signature key (Identity does not expose it); the hub does not check
    it.
  - Room names are first come, first served at the hub: /create of a name
    someone else used adds you to their room's participant list at the hub.
  - BasicCredential only, and nothing authenticates the hub or peers beyond
    the proxy's mTLS. The state directory holds private keys in the clear,
    protected only by its modes.

Generated files (target/, state directories) are not in the repository.
